---
title: "Free Tool to Blur Faces in a Video Online"
description: "Find and track every face in a video and blur it. The new video keeps its sound. Runs in your browser, so your video is never uploaded."
faq:
  - q: "Is the Video Face Blur tool free?"
    a: "Yes. The tool is free, with no account, no sign-up, no watermark and no limit on how often you use it."
  - q: "Is my video uploaded to a server?"
    a: "No. The page has no server code. Face detection, tracking and making the new video all happen in your browser, and your video never leaves your device. The only downloads are the small face model and the code that runs it, which contain no user data."
  - q: "Can someone undo the blur?"
    a: "No. In each frame, the tool shrinks the face area to a few pixels and stretches it back, or fills it with black, before it saves the frame. The new video does not contain the original faces, so there is nothing to recover. At a low strength, a blur can still show the rough shape of a face, so use a high strength or the Black box style when it matters."
  - q: "Does it blur every face in every frame?"
    a: "No automatic tool is perfect. Faces that are very small, turned away or moving fast can be missed for a moment. Play the preview to the end before you share the video. You can show or hide each person in the People list."
  - q: "Does the new video keep its sound?"
    a: "Yes, when \"Keep audio\" is on. Turn it off to make a video with no sound. If your browser cannot convert the audio, the video is made without sound and the tool tells you."
  - q: "Can I keep one person's face visible?"
    a: "Yes. After the scan, the People list shows a picture of each person the tool found. Click a person to show their face in the whole video. Click again to hide it."
  - q: "Which video formats and lengths does it support?"
    a: "MP4, MOV and WebM files up to 10 minutes long. Some browsers can also read other formats, such as MKV. The new video is always an MP4 file. Videos larger than 1080p are made smaller to 1080p."
  - q: "How long does it take?"
    a: "Scanning and making the video take about one to two times the video's length on a laptop. Phones are slower. The tool shows how much time is left while it scans."
---

